1. **composite_controls gauge**: The runtime gauge now has `data-max=100 data-min=0` (good) but the inner bar is still `width:4200%`. The handler computes `max = Number(el.querySelector(".pathland-gauge")?.dataset.max ?? 1)`. But `el` IS the gauge; `el.querySelector(".pathland-gauge")` searches descendants — the gauge is the root, not a descendant. So `dataset.max` on the queried (nonexistent) descendant → null → max=1 → 4200%. 

   The PROP_VALUE gauge code uses `el.querySelector(".pathland-gauge")` — since el itself is the gauge, I need to check `el.matches(".pathland-gauge")` first. Let me fix the gauge part of PROP_VALUE:
   ```
   const gauge = el.matches(".pathland-gauge") ? el : el.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".pathland-gauge");
   if (gauge) {
     const max = Number(gauge.dataset.max ?? 1);
     const min = Number(gauge.dataset.min ?? 0);
     ...
   }
   ```

2. **layout**: Expected has `inset:0;position:absolute` on the zstack wrappers; Received has `position:absolute` only (no inset). Wait — the Received wrapper shows `style:position:absolute` but no `inset:0`. My `placedChild` sets `wrapper.style.inset = "0"`. happy-dom may drop `inset`?? Or serialize differently. Let me check — the earlier (before placedChild existed, first failing run) the wrappers also showed `position:absolute` without inset. So happy-dom drops `inset`? Let me test: `el.style.inset = "0"` in happy-dom → getAttribute("style")? Possibly happy-dom doesn't support `inset` and drops it. But the SSR side (parsed) preserves `inset:0` raw. So asymmetric again.

   Hmm. `inset` is a relatively new CSS property. happy-dom might not support it. Options:
   (a) Set the wrapper styles via the style attribute (setStyleCss-like) for the zstack wrapper: `position:absolute;inset:0`. That preserves in happy-dom and browsers.
   (b) Normalize in the test: expand `inset:0` → top/right/bottom/left 0? No — simpler to make the runtime preserve it.

   Actually — the cleanest is to set the wrapper's styles via the attribute, mirroring SSR. Let me update `placedChild`:
   ```ts
   const wrapper = document.createElement("div");
   wrapper.setAttribute("style", "position:absolute;inset:0");
   wrapper.appendChild(child);
   ```
   That exactly matches the SSR `<div style="position:absolute;inset:0">`. And it's robust in browsers + happy-dom. 

   Similarly the ZSTACK shell in elements.ts sets position:relative;width/height via CSSOM — those serialize fine (relative/100% supported). But `inset` is the problem. For consistency, I could use the attribute for the wrapper only (the divergence case). 

   But wait — will mixing attribute-based style and subsequent CSSOM edits on the wrapper cause issues? The wrapper isn't in the registry; no deltas target it. Safe.

